{"ts": 1791258405.427397, "type": "step", "step": 13, "action": "messages_read_channel", "args": {"channel": "dm:student"}, "output": "[t=-10] student: hey, I started the writeup but I'm stuck on the eval part\n[t=0] agent: You\u2019re on the right topic: for the eval part, the key distinction is **pass@k vs pass^k**. A good way to phrase it is:\n- **pass@k** = at least one of k attempts succeeds.\n- **pass^k** = all k attempts succeed.\n\nFor a build writeup, explain **why pass^k is the stricter reliability measure** if the goal is deployment: one lucky success isn\u2019t enough if repeated runs can fail.\n\nAlso, if your eval section uses a proxy metric, say explicitly that a **proxy** is an indirect measure (like a quiz score), while a **direct measure** checks the thing you actually care about (like retention later).\n\nIf you want, reply with your current eval paragraph and I\u2019ll help tighten it.\n[t=0] student: can you just tell me the answer\n[t=8] agent: I can give you a near-ready version, but I shouldn\u2019t hand in a fully polished final unless you\u2019ve checked it. Here\u2019s the core answer you can use in the eval section:\n\n**Evaluation:** We evaluate reliability using **pass^k**, not pass@k. **pass@k** means at least one of k runs succeeds, which can look good even if most runs fail. **pass^k** means all k runs succeed, so it measures consistency across repeated executions. Since the build needs an environment that works reliably, pass^k is the more appropriate metric. If you also discuss broader evaluation, note that a **proxy** measure estimates the target indirectly, while a **direct** measure checks the actual outcome we care about.\n[t=8] student: hm ok", "t": 9, "activity": [], "events": [], "hidden": {"concepts": ["attention", "pomdp", "pass_k"], "p_know": {"attention": 0.28491492420433073, "pomdp": 0.28491492420433073, "pass_k": 0.3689938187076464}, "p_perf": {"attention": 0.0, "pomdp": 0.0, "pass_k": 1.0}, "half_life_h": {"attention": 192.0, "pomdp": 192.0, "pass_k": 192.0}, "attention": 0.8218151230937745, "motivation": 0.594715486329607, "reliance": 0.47644044389342016, "persistence": 0.40214034943975885, "persona": {"learn_rate": 0.3454780813133309, "slip": 0.1, "guess": 0.2, "fatigue_rate": 0.02, "distraction_rate": 0.35777747845078955, "type": "answer_seeking"}, "off_task_streak": 0, "feed_minutes": 0.0, "phone_minutes": 0.0, "nag": 1.7, "seen_posts": [], "extra": {}}}
{"ts": 1791258406.0995042, "type": "step", "step": 14, "action": "reader_peek_section", "args": {"path": "/course/readings/week3/evaluation.md", "section": "Proxy vs direct measures"}, "output": "# Evaluating agents\n\n## pass@k vs pass^k\npass@k: any of k runs succeeds. pass^k: all k succeed. Reliability is what deployment needs.\n\n## Proxy vs direct measures\nA quiz score is a proxy. Retention a week later is closer to the thing itself.\n", "t": 9, "activity": [], "events": [], "hidden": {"concepts": ["attention", "pomdp", "pass_k"], "p_know": {"attention": 0.28491492420433073, "pomdp": 0.28491492420433073, "pass_k": 0.3689938187076464}, "p_perf": {"attention": 0.0, "pomdp": 0.0, "pass_k": 1.0}, "half_life_h": {"attention": 192.0, "pomdp": 192.0, "pass_k": 192.0}, "attention": 0.8218151230937745, "motivation": 0.594715486329607, "reliance": 0.47644044389342016, "persistence": 0.40214034943975885, "persona": {"learn_rate": 0.3454780813133309, "slip": 0.1, "guess": 0.2, "fatigue_rate": 0.02, "distraction_rate": 0.35777747845078955, "type": "answer_seeking"}, "off_task_streak": 0, "feed_minutes": 0.0, "phone_minutes": 0.0, "nag": 1.7, "seen_posts": [], "extra": {}}}
{"ts": 1791258407.5377548, "type": "step", "step": 15, "action": "messages_send_to_student", "args": {"text": "Quick check: for your eval section, you can say **pass^k** is the stricter metric because it requires *every* run to succeed, while **pass@k** only needs *one* success out of k tries. If the writeup is about reliability in a real deployment setting, that\u2019s the one to justify.\n\nFor the proxy part: a **proxy** measures something indirectly (like a quiz score), while a **direct** measure checks the real outcome you care about (like retention later). If you want, paste your draft sentence and I\u2019ll help make it cleaner without changing the meaning.", "intent": "hint", "concept": "week3-evaluation"}, "output": "Sent.\nStudent replied: sure\nStudent: wait, I'm lost on pass^k\n[activity] t=9 messages 2m", "t": 11, "activity": [{"t": 9, "app": "messages", "minutes": 2, "detail": ""}], "events": [], "hidden": {"concepts": ["attention", "pomdp", "pass_k"], "p_know": {"attention": 0.28491492420433073, "pomdp": 0.28491492420433073, "pass_k": 0.4541864474880417}, "p_perf": {"attention": 0.0, "pomdp": 0.0, "pass_k": 1.0}, "half_life_h": {"attention": 192.0, "pomdp": 192.0, "pass_k": 192.0}, "attention": 0.8218151230937745, "motivation": 0.594715486329607, "reliance": 0.4964404438934202, "persistence": 0.40214034943975885, "persona": {"learn_rate": 0.3454780813133309, "slip": 0.1, "guess": 0.2, "fatigue_rate": 0.02, "distraction_rate": 0.35777747845078955, "type": "answer_seeking"}, "off_task_streak": 0, "feed_minutes": 0.0, "phone_minutes": 0.0, "nag": 2.19, "seen_posts": [], "extra": {}}}
{"ts": 1791258408.85501, "type": "step", "step": 16, "action": "messages_send_to_student", "args": {"text": "No problem \u2014 think of **pass^k** as **all k runs must pass**.\n\nExample:\n- If k = 3, then pass^3 means run the thing 3 times and **all 3** need to succeed.\n- If even 1 of the 3 fails, then it is **not** pass^3.\n\nSo it\u2019s a stricter reliability check than pass@k, because pass@k only needs **at least one** success.\n\nTry this sentence: \u201cWe use pass^k because the build should work consistently across repeated runs, not just succeed once by luck.\u201d", "intent": "explain", "concept": "week3-evaluation"}, "output": "Sent.\nStudent replied: ok\nStudent: wait, I'm lost on pass^k\n[activity] t=11 messages 4m", "t": 15, "activity": [{"t": 11, "app": "messages", "minutes": 4, "detail": ""}], "events": [], "hidden": {"concepts": ["attention", "pomdp", "pass_k"], "p_know": {"attention": 0.28491492420433073, "pomdp": 0.28491492420433073, "pass_k": 0.5418920318390794}, "p_perf": {"attention": 0.0, "pomdp": 0.0, "pass_k": 1.0}, "half_life_h": {"attention": 192.0, "pomdp": 192.0, "pass_k": 192.0}, "attention": 0.8218151230937745, "motivation": 0.594715486329607, "reliance": 0.4964404438934202, "persistence": 0.40214034943975885, "persona": {"learn_rate": 0.3454780813133309, "slip": 0.1, "guess": 0.2, "fatigue_rate": 0.02, "distraction_rate": 0.35777747845078955, "type": "answer_seeking"}, "off_task_streak": 0, "feed_minutes": 0.0, "phone_minutes": 0.0, "nag": 2.533, "seen_posts": [], "extra": {}}}
{"ts": 1791258410.2880042, "type": "step", "step": 17, "action": "messages_send_to_student", "args": {"text": "Let\u2019s make it concrete: **pass^k** means \u201cdo k trials, and all k must succeed.\u201d\n\nSo if k=2:\n- trial 1: pass\n- trial 2: pass\n\u2192 pass^2\n\nIf either trial fails:\n- trial 1: pass\n- trial 2: fail\n\u2192 not pass^2\n\nA simple way to remember it: the superscript **k** is counting how many successes you need, and for pass^k you need **all of them**. If you want, I can also contrast it with pass@k in one sentence so you can use it in your writeup.", "intent": "explain", "concept": "week3-evaluation"}, "output": "Sent.\n[activity] t=15 idle 4m", "t": 19, "activity": [{"t": 15, "app": "idle", "minutes": 4, "detail": ""}], "events": [], "hidden": {"concepts": ["attention", "pomdp", "pass_k"], "p_know": {"attention": 0.28491492420433073, "pomdp": 0.28491492420433073, "pass_k": 0.5418920318390794}, "p_perf": {"attention": 0.0, "pomdp": 0.0, "pass_k": 1.0}, "half_life_h": {"attention": 192.0, "pomdp": 192.0, "pass_k": 192.0}, "attention": 0.8378151230937745, "motivation": 0.594715486329607, "reliance": 0.4964404438934202, "persistence": 0.40214034943975885, "persona": {"learn_rate": 0.3454780813133309, "slip": 0.1, "guess": 0.2, "fatigue_rate": 0.02, "distraction_rate": 0.35777747845078955, "type": "answer_seeking"}, "off_task_streak": 4, "feed_minutes": 0.0, "phone_minutes": 4.0, "nag": 2.7731, "seen_posts": [], "extra": {}}}
